<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      Permission surface is deliberately minimal and stays that way.

      ⛔ DO NOT ADD READ_SMS or RECEIVE_SMS. The inbox reads messages from the
         District AI platform API, never from the device. Those are restricted
         permissions available only to a default SMS handler, and requesting them
         would trigger a Play policy review this app cannot pass.
      ⛔ DO NOT ADD READ_CONTACTS. The Google and Outlook contact importers run
         server-side.
      ⛔ DO NOT ADD location permissions. Nothing in the product needs them.

      RECORD_AUDIO and CAMERA landed with multi-party rooms. MANAGE_OWN_CALLS landed
      with the outbound softphone and is the whole of the "calling" surface — see its
      own note below for why the foreground-service permissions did NOT come with it.
      Every addition here costs a Play Data Safety declaration and, for foreground
      service types, a demo video.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!--
      Multi-party rooms.

      ⛔ BOTH ARE RUNTIME PERMISSIONS AND BOTH ARE OPTIONAL TO THE FEATURE. A room is joinable
         with neither: no camera is audio-only attendance, and no microphone is listen-only
         attendance — which is exactly the seat the server gives a `viewer` regardless, since
         its token carries canPublish:false. The room screen degrades in two directions rather
         than refusing to open, and says on screen which control is off and why.
      ⛔ DECLARED HERE, REQUESTED AT THE ROOM. The request is made by the ACTIVE_ROOM
         destination immediately before it connects — never at launch. A permission dialog with
         no visible reason is the one users deny permanently.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />

    <!--
      ⛔ `required="false"` ON ALL THREE, AND THIS IS A DISTRIBUTION DECISION RATHER THAN A
         FORMALITY. Declaring CAMERA in <uses-permission> makes Play IMPLY
         `android.hardware.camera` as REQUIRED unless it is declared optional here — which would
         hide this app from every Chromebook, tablet and TV without one, for a feature that
         works fine without a camera. `camera.any` and `camera.autofocus` are implied the same
         way. The app must check availability at runtime rather than assume; the room already
         does, because it treats a denied camera as an ordinary way to attend.
    -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera" android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera.any" android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera.autofocus" android:required="false" />
    <!--
      ⚠️ Same reasoning for the microphone: RECORD_AUDIO implies `android.hardware.microphone`
         as required, and a listen-only participant needs none.
    -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.microphone" android:required="false" />

    <!--
      The outbound softphone.

      ⛔ MANAGE_OWN_CALLS IS AN INSTALL-TIME ("normal") PERMISSION, NOT A RUNTIME ONE, and
         confusing the two is how a Telecom integration ends up with a request dialog that can
         never appear. Nothing asks the user for this; it is granted at install and it is what
         lets this app register a SELF-MANAGED PhoneAccount and call TelecomManager.placeCall.
         The only runtime permission the softphone needs is RECORD_AUDIO, declared above.
      ⛔ IT IS NOT CALL_PHONE AND MUST NOT BECOME IT. CALL_PHONE places calls on the CELLULAR
         network through the default dialer — a second, real, billed call while this app is
         already connected over SIP — and it is a runtime permission in the restricted Phone
         group that Play scrutinises. MANAGE_OWN_CALLS says the opposite thing: this app manages
         its own calls and wants the OS merely to know one is happening.

      ⚠️ THE FOREGROUND-SERVICE PERMISSIONS ARRIVED WITH INBOUND CALLING, EXACTLY AS THIS NOTE
         SAID THEY WOULD — see the block below. The reasoning that kept them out is unchanged and
         still describes the OUTBOUND path: a call dialled from the in-call screen is screen-on by
         construction and needs nothing to keep the process alive. An ANSWERED INBOUND call is the
         case the note was reserving them for: the user puts the phone to their ear, the Activity
         stops, and a process with no foreground service is one Android is entitled to freeze
         mid-conversation.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MANAGE_OWN_CALLS" />

    <!--
      Inbound calling: the foreground service, and the notification that rings.

      ⛔ FOUR PERMISSIONS AND EACH ONE IS LOAD-BEARING; NONE IS A PAIR WITH ANOTHER.
         FOREGROUND_SERVICE is the API 28 base grant. FOREGROUND_SERVICE_PHONE_CALL and
         FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE are the API 34 per-TYPE grants, and Android 14 requires the
         app to HOLD the permission matching every type its service declares — a service typed
         `phoneCall|microphone` while holding only the first throws the moment the mic is used.
         POST_NOTIFICATIONS is the API 33 runtime permission without which nothing this app posts
         is visible at all.
      ⛔ FOREGROUND_SERVICE_PHONE_CALL COSTS A PLAY DEMO VIDEO AT REVIEW and a written
         justification for MANAGE_OWN_CALLS alongside it. That is a Console obligation this
         declaration creates, not an optional follow-up.
      ⛔ AND THE MICROPHONE TYPE IS NOT REDUNDANT WITH RECORD_AUDIO. RECORD_AUDIO is permission to
         use the microphone at all; FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE is permission to keep using it
         while the app is in the background, which is the whole point of the service.
      ⚠️ POST_NOTIFICATIONS IS DECLARED HERE AND REQUESTED AT THE FIRST POST-SIGN-IN LANDING, never
         at launch — the same rule RECORD_AUDIO follows at the room and at the dialler. A denial is
         not fatal: `notify` becomes a silent no-op, the Telecom connection still rings and the call
         is still answerable from the OS's own call surfaces.
      ⛔ THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO USE_FULL_SCREEN_INTENT. Its auto-grant requires the app's core
         functionality to be calling, and a nineteen-section business dashboard will not clear that
         bar — so on Android 14+ a full-screen intent would be DEMOTED to the heads-up notification
         this app draws anyway, and the app would carry a permission it was refused for nothing. The
         heads-up Answer/Decline path is the guaranteed one, and it is the one that is built.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_PHONE_CALL" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
         ⛔ REMOVED, NOT DECLARED, AND IT IS NOT OURS TO BEGIN WITH. The LiveKit SDK's
         own manifest contributes FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MEDIA_PROJECTION and a
         ScreenCaptureService through manifest merging, so a release bundle asks for
         screen capture even though this app has no screen sharing: nothing in the
         Kotlin tree references screenCapture, mediaProjection or screenShare, and
         nothing ever starts that service.
         ⚠️ THE COST OF LEAVING IT IS NOT THEORETICAL. Play requires a written
         justification and usually a demo video for every foreground service type, and
         media projection is among the types it scrutinises hardest — so an unused
         dependency permission turns into describing a feature that does not exist.
         ⚠️ VERIFY ON THE BUILT AAB, NEVER ON THIS FILE. A merge rule that silently
         matches nothing looks identical to one that works; the only honest check is
         dumping the merged manifest out of the bundle.
         🔑 If screen sharing is ever built, delete both rules rather than working
         around them.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MEDIA_PROJECTION"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <!--
      ⚠️ OPTIONAL, for the same distribution reason the camera is. Play infers hardware features
         from the permission set, and a "calling" app that required telephony hardware would
         vanish from every tablet and Chromebook — devices on which this softphone works
         perfectly, because it places calls over the data network and never touches the radio.
    -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.telephony" android:required="false" />

    <!--
      ⛔ WITHOUT THIS, `CustomTabsLauncher.browserPackage` RETURNS NULL ON EVERY DEVICE RUNNING
         API 30 OR LATER, AND IT FAILS SILENTLY. Package visibility filtering hides every other
         installed app from `resolveActivity` unless it is declared here, so the probe would find
         no browser, `launchExternally` would report "no browser" on a phone that has three, and
         the marketplace hand-off would show an error instead of opening the web page.
      ⛔ AND THE REASON A BROWSER HAS TO BE NAMED AT ALL IS App Links: this app is a verified
         handler for `https://[www.]distronode.com/dashboard/district...`, so an IMPLICIT
         ACTION_VIEW for one of those URLs resolves back into this app rather than out to a
         browser. See the ⛔ in CustomTabsLauncher's header.
      ⚠️ BOTH SCHEMES, BECAUSE THE PROBE IS `http://`. A browser's catch-all filter declares http
         and https separately, and a queries entry has to match the filter it is asking about.
         Neither entry can match this app: its own web filter is https-only and is bound to two
         named hosts with a path prefix.
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
            <data android:scheme="http" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
            <data android:scheme="https" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <application
        android:name=".DistrictApplication"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.DistrictAI"
        tools:targetApi="36"
        xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

        <!--
          ⛔ SENTRY'S OWN ContentProvider MUST BE TOLD NOT TO INITIALISE THE SDK, AND WITHOUT THIS
             LINE IT INITIALISES IT **BEFORE** `Application.onCreate` WITH NO DSN. This is not a
             theory: `sentry-android-core-8.53.0.aar` declares
             `io.sentry.android.core.SentryInitProvider` in its own manifest, the merger adds it
             here, and a ContentProvider's `onCreate` runs before the Application's. It calls
             `SentryAndroid.init` reading configuration from manifest meta-data — of which this app
             publishes none — so the SDK comes up ARMED AND POINTED NOWHERE, and
             `DistrictSentry.initialize` then re-initialises it a second time a moment later.
          ⛔ ON A DEVICE THE DOUBLE INIT IS SILENT: crashes still report, because the second init
             wins. The costs are a wasted provider init on every cold start and a window in which
             events would have gone to a DSN-less client.
          ⚠️ NO UNIT TEST GUARDS THIS LINE. Under Robolectric `Sentry.isEnabled()` reads true
             whatever this meta-data says, so `DistrictSentryGuardTest` asserts the return value of
             `DistrictSentry.initialize` instead (see the note there), and deleting this line would
             leave it green. Treat this meta-data as reviewed-by-hand.
          ⚠️ `SentryPerformanceProvider` (the sibling provider, initOrder 200) IS DELIBERATELY LEFT
             IN PLACE. It is not gated by this flag — it collects app-start timings — and it is
             what lets an ANR report say whether the ANR happened during startup. Removing it with
             `tools:node="remove"` would trade a real ANR diagnostic for a few milliseconds, and
             tracing being off means nothing it collects is billed.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="io.sentry.auto-init"
            android:value="false" />

        <!--
          ⛔ `launchMode="singleTask"` IS REQUIRED FOR THE LOGIN CALLBACK, not a preference.
          The PKCE callback arrives as a fresh VIEW intent from the system browser. Under the
          default `standard` mode that would create a SECOND MainActivity instance on top of the
          existing one, and the new instance's NativeLoginFlow would have no pending attempt —
          so `completeLogin` would return NoAttemptInProgress and login would fail every time,
          with nothing obviously wrong. singleTask routes the intent to the LIVE instance via
          onNewIntent, where the in-memory verifier still exists.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.DistrictAI"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              The native login callback.

              ⚠️ A CUSTOM SCHEME IS CLAIMABLE BY ANY APP ON THE DEVICE, which is exactly why the
              flow does not trust it: PKCE makes an intercepted code useless, and the `state`
              check refuses a callback this app did not start. The verified App Link form
              (https://www.distronode.com/auth/native/callback) is stronger and is already
              allowlisted server-side, but it cannot be verified until Play holds the app
              signing key, so it can only be adopted after the first upload. Both forms are in
              NATIVE_REDIRECT_ALLOWLIST, so switching needs no server change.
            -->
            <intent-filter android:autoVerify="false">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data
                    android:host="auth"
                    android:scheme="districtai" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              Android App Links: a dashboard URL opens the app instead of a browser tab.

              ⛔ `autoVerify="true"` IS A REQUEST, NOT A GRANT, AND IT FAILS CLOSED. At install the
                 OS fetches https://<each host>/.well-known/assetlinks.json and checks that a
                 statement names THIS package with the SHA-256 of the certificate that ACTUALLY
                 signed the installed app. If the statement is missing, unreachable, served as
                 anything but application/json, or reached through a redirect, verification fails
                 and every link here opens in a browser exactly as it does today. Nothing breaks;
                 the feature simply does not switch on. That is why this can ship before the Play
                 Console entry exists.
              ⛔ THE STATEMENT LIST IS ALREADY LIVE AND IS STILL INCOMPLETE. The server half is
                 the server's `/.well-known/assetlinks.json` route, which publishes TWO
                 statements: the release package against the UPLOAD key, and
                 `com.distronode.districtai.debug` against a development debug key. The debug
                 statement is what makes on-device verification testable NOW. ⛔ But Play App
                 Signing RE-SIGNS every upload with a key Google holds, so an app installed FROM
                 PLAY is signed by a certificate that appears nowhere in that file yet — its
                 SHA-256 must be read from Console → Setup → App integrity → App signing and
                 APPENDED (not substituted) after the first upload. Until then, verification works
                 for sideloaded builds and fails for Play installs.

              ⛔ THE PATH SCOPE IS `/dashboard/district` AND MUST NOT BE WIDENED. A `pathPrefix`
                 claim is a promise to HANDLE every URL it matches, and a claimed URL the app
                 cannot render is a dead end the user cannot escape by pressing back — worse than
                 a browser tab. Everything under this prefix resolves to a real destination or to
                 the overview (see AppLinkResolver); nothing else is claimed. In particular the
                 apex marketing pages, `/pricing`, `/blog` and — importantly — `/auth/...` are NOT
                 claimed, so the native login callback keeps arriving through the custom scheme
                 above and cannot be swallowed by this filter.
              ⚠️ BOTH HOSTS, AND `distronode.com` IS NOT REDUNDANT WITH `www.`. Android matches the
                 host literally, with no notion that one redirects to the other, so a link shared
                 as the apex form would open a browser without this entry. Each host is verified
                 independently and each must serve the statement list without a redirect — the
                 route handler's own header documents why the apex does.
              ⛔ `distronode.ca` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. It is a separate registrable domain with
                 its own zone and its own canonical-host rules, so claiming it needs a statement
                 list published on THAT host too; adding the host here without that would just be
                 a filter that never verifies. It is deferred until that list exists.
              ⚠️ `android:scheme="https"` ONLY. An `http` entry would let a downgraded link claim
                 the app, and App Links verification only ever applies to https.
            -->
            <intent-filter android:autoVerify="true">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="https" />
                <data android:host="distronode.com" />
                <data android:host="www.distronode.com" />
                <data android:pathPrefix="/dashboard/district" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          The self-managed ConnectionService behind the softphone.

          ⛔ `android:permission` HERE IS A REQUIREMENT ON THE BINDER, NOT A PERMISSION THIS APP
             HOLDS, and that inversion is the whole point of the line. BIND_TELECOM_CONNECTION_
             SERVICE is signature-level and only the platform holds it, so this declaration says
             "only the OS may bind me" — which is what stops another installed app binding this
             service and driving this app's calls. Omitting it does not merely weaken the
             service: Telecom refuses to use a ConnectionService that does not require it, and
             every placeCall fails with nothing in the app to point at.
          ⛔ `exported="true"` IS REQUIRED FOR THE SAME REASON AND IS NOT A WEAKENING. The
             binder is a different process (the system's), so an unexported service is
             unreachable by the only caller that is allowed to reach it. The permission above is
             what makes exporting safe.
          ⚠️ The intent action is the framework's fixed contract; Telecom discovers the service
             by querying for it, not from the PhoneAccountHandle's ComponentName alone.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".telecom.DistrictConnectionService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_TELECOM_CONNECTION_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.telecom.ConnectionService" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!--
          FCM's entry point into this app.

          ⛔ MANIFEST-DECLARED AND `exported="false"`. The system binds this through the Play
             Services messaging library running IN THIS PROCESS, not across a process boundary, so
             it needs no export — and exporting it would let any app on the device deliver a
             synthetic push, which on the incoming-call branch means making this phone ring.
          ⛔ THE ACTION IS THE SDK'S FIXED CONTRACT. `FirebaseMessaging` discovers the handler by
             querying for exactly this intent filter; a service without it is never called and the
             failure is complete silence rather than an error.
          ⚠️ NO `google-services` PLUGIN AND NO google-services.json ANYWHERE IN THIS BUILD.
             Firebase is initialised by hand in DistrictApplication; see DistrictFirebase for the
             three reasons.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".push.DistrictFirebaseMessagingService"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.google.firebase.MESSAGING_EVENT" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!--
          The foreground service that keeps the process alive for the duration of an answered call.

          ⛔ `foregroundServiceType="phoneCall|microphone"` AND BOTH HALVES ARE REQUIRED ON API 34+.
             `phoneCall` is what this is; `microphone` is what it does, because the media engine
             publishes an audio track the whole time. Declaring only one of them either throws when
             the mic is used or misdescribes a telephone call to the reviewer who has to approve it.
          ⛔ `exported="false"`: it is started by this app and by nothing else. An exported call
             service is a way for another app to put a "call in progress" notification on screen.
          ⚠️ Its manifest type must stay in step with the two FOREGROUND_SERVICE_* permissions
             above; Android 14 checks the pair at start time, not at install.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".call.CallForegroundService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="phoneCall|microphone" />

        <!--
          The Decline button on the ringing notification.

          ⛔ `exported="false"` AND ADDRESSED BY COMPONENT. The action string is this app's own, but
             an implicit broadcast would be delivered to any app that registered for it — and, on
             API 26+, would not reach a manifest-registered receiver at all, so the feature would
             fail in the ordinary case and leak the call id in the exotic one.
          ⚠️ A BROADCAST RATHER THAN AN ACTIVITY BECAUSE DECLINING HAS NOTHING TO SHOW. Answering
             does, which is why that action names MainActivity directly.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".call.IncomingCallActionReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />
        <!--
             ⛔ The LiveKit SDK's screen-capture service, removed for the reason on the
             FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MEDIA_PROJECTION rule above. Removing the permission
             alone would leave a declared service whose type the app has no grant for.
             ⚠️ `tools:ignore="MissingClass"` IS REQUIRED AND IS NOT A SUPPRESSION OF A
             REAL PROBLEM. lint resolves `android:name` against the DEBUG classpath and
             fails with MissingClass because it cannot see this class; the node exists
             here only to be deleted at merge time, so "the class is missing" is the
             intended end state rather than a defect. Without it `lintDebug` errors and
             fails the build.
        -->
        <service
            android:name="io.livekit.android.room.track.screencapture.ScreenCaptureService"
            tools:node="remove"
            tools:ignore="MissingClass" />

    </application>

</manifest>
